Step 1: Create a project directory
mkdir -p ~/helm-practical
cd ~/helm-practical

Step 2: Create the chart
helm create my-nginx

Step 3: Inspect its directory structure:
ls -R my-nginx

You should see a structure resembling:

my-nginx/
├── Chart.yaml
├── values.yaml
├── charts/
├── templates/
│   ├── deployment.yaml
│   ├── service.yaml
│   ├── ingress.yaml
│   ├── hpa.yaml
│   ├── serviceaccount.yaml
│   ├── _helpers.tpl
│   ├── NOTES.txt
│   └── tests/
└── .helmignore

The exact generated files can differ between Helm versions.

Step 4:  Understand the files
File or directory	Purpose
Chart.yaml	Chart metadata
values.yaml	Default chart configuration
templates/	Kubernetes resource templates
templates/deployment.yaml	Deployment definition
templates/service.yaml	Service definition
templates/ingress.yaml	Optional Ingress definition
templates/hpa.yaml	Optional autoscaling definition
templates/_helpers.tpl	Reusable template functions
templates/NOTES.txt	Instructions displayed after deployment
charts/	Chart dependencies
.helmignore	Files to exclude from packaged charts
Teaching point

The templates describe the Kubernetes resources.

The values configure those templates.

A release is an installed instance of the chart.
